<section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
            <span class="eyebrow">First, don't</span>
            <h2>Don't panic-fix one thing.</h2>
            <p class="lede">When something surfaces, the instinct is to change one password, delete one app, or pull one device offline. In a high-stakes situation that can leave the real exposure in place — and, if the timing is wrong, tip off the other side. One visible problem rarely tells you the full picture.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
            <span class="eyebrow">Why</span>
            <h2>Why &ldquo;maybe it was a hack&rdquo; isn't where to start.</h2>
            <p class="lede">Hacking is only one possibility, and usually not the likeliest. What someone knows may come from ordinary access, a setting, a shared service, a recovery path, or an online trail — not a break-in. Until the whole picture is reviewed, guessing which one it was just costs you time you may not have.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

